                            },
                            {
                                "box": {
                                    "id": "obj-33",
                                    "linecount": 87,
                                    "maxclass": "comment",
                                    "numinlets": 1,
                                    "numoutlets": 0,
                                    "patching_rect": [ 30.0, 553.0, 577.0, 1274.0 ],
                                    "text": "================================================================================\nharmonizer_minishift_doubler — Parameter Information\n================================================================================\n\nOVERVIEW\nA microshift (Soundtoys) / micropitch (Eventide) style doubler: the audio\ninput is copied twice and each copy is pitch-shifted by a few cents in\nopposite directions, one per side. Unlike a chorus the detune is a fixed\npitch offset rather than a delay-time wobble (motion only breathes its size\nslowly, and can be set to 0), so the copies beat slowly against the dry\naudio and against each other (DAFx-08 Paper 30 Sec. 3-4), and because the\ntwo sides drift apart in pitch they decorrelate, which is what produces the\nwidth (DAFx-24 Paper 92 Sec. 1). The\nresult is a thick, wide double with no audible pitch of its own, made for a\nlive bass that has to fill a big rig.\n\nINLETS & OUTLETS\n- in1 / in2: Stereo Audio Input. Voice A shifts the left input up, voice\n  B shifts the right input down. If in2 stays silent for 250 ms, in1 is used\n  for both sides.\n- in3: Pitch f0 in Hz, used only to size the grains so the splices land on a\n  period. 0 or out-of-range (outside 20..1200 Hz) holds the last valid value;\n  until the first valid value arrives fallback_hz is used (and follows the\n  knob). There is no pitch tracker and in3 is never divided by.\n- in4: One-Bar Phasor [0..1] (an exception to the repo's Hz convention).\n  Drives the detune breathing, one cycle per bar. 100 ms after in4 stops\n  moving (unpatched, frozen, or held at any constant), an internal slow\n  oscillator (0.13 Hz, ~7.7 s per cycle) drives it instead; set motion to 0\n  for a completely static microshift.\n- out1 / out2: Stereo Output (dry crossfaded with the doubled pair)\n\nPARAMETERS (6)\n\n1. detune_cents\n   - Range: 0 to 40 cents (Default: 12)\n   - Taper: Linear, one voice up and one down by this amount.\n   - Function: How far each copy is detuned. Small values (5-10 cents) give a\n     subtle widening that still reads as one instrument; 15-25 cents give the classic\n     Eventide-style double; 30-40 cents are audibly out of tune, useful as a\n     sour, unstable layer under a riddim bass.\n\n2. pre_delay_ms\n   - Range: 0 to 30 ms (Default: 12)\n   - Taper: Linear.\n   - Function: Delays the doubled pair behind the dry signal, so the copies read\n     as a second instrument rather than as a comb on top of the first. Very small\n     values (0-3 ms) are the tightest, most comb-like doubling. The grains\n     themselves add between 0 and one f0 period on top (0-12 ms at 82 Hz).\n\n3. width\n   - Range: 0 to 100 % (Default: 70)\n   - Taper: Mid/side crossfade between the two voices.\n   - Function: 0 puts both detunes in the centre (a mono double), 100 puts voice\n     A fully left and voice B fully right. The mid component is untouched, so\n     the effect stays mono-compatible.\n\n4. mix\n   - Range: 0 to 100 % (Default: 50)\n   - Taper: Linear dry-to-double crossfade.\n   - Function: How much of the widened pair replaces the dry audio.\n\n5. motion\n   - Range: 0 to 100 % (Default: 20)\n   - Taper: Linear depth, one bar per cycle.\n   - Function: Breathes the detune spread: both voices move apart and back\n     together (A up/B down, then towards unison) by up to this % of\n     detune_cents, so the width opens and closes once per bar instead of\n     sitting still. At 100 the spread swings between unison and twice\n     detune_cents. At 0 the detune is constant. The phase comes from in4 when\n     the phasor moves, otherwise from the internal slow oscillator.\n\n6. fallback_hz\n   - Range: 40 to 300 Hz (Default: 82)\n   - Taper: Linear.\n   - Function: f0 assumed until in3 first reports a pitch; it only sets the grain\n     length, so the effect is fully usable with no tracker at all (set it near\n     the register you play in). Once in3 has reported a pitch, the last valid\n     pitch is held and this knob is ignored.\n\nPORT NOTES\n- No pitch tracking and no delay-time modulation: the detune is a pitch offset\n  (optionally breathing via motion), which is what separates this from the\n  chorus effects in this repo.\n- Each grain keeps the length it started with, so a note change on in3 never\n  cuts a window short (no splice clicks on note changes).\n- At detune_cents = 0 the two voices are identical and the wet is just a delayed\n  copy of the dry; use mix or a non-zero detune.\n",
                                    "varname": "paraminfo_comment"
                                }
                            }
                        ],
